    "title": "Judge to hear arguments in Mobile council lawsuit - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Gracie King ,\n1 hours ago\nEditor’s Note: Here’s the latest update on this story: Mobile City Councilman William Carroll sues Samantha Ingram, contests District 2 election results\nMOBILE, Ala. ( WKRG ) — A judge will hear arguments over a lawsuit filed over the results of the election for Mobile’s second city council district on Thursday, Oct. 2.\nUPDATE: New video shows official punching suspect in head during immigration operation Samantha Ingram is seeking to dismiss the lawsuit filed by current District Two councilman William Carroll on Sept. 5.\nIngram won August’s election for District Two, but Carroll argues Ingram did not meet the residency requirement\nIngram left Alabama in 2007 and later lived in Georgia, the lawsuit says. The suit alleges that Ingram did not re-establish legal residence in Alabama in the 12 months before election day.\nAccording to Alabama Code 11-44C-17, a council member must have lived in the city they represent at least a year before the election, and must live in that district for at least 90 days before voting day.\nAdditionally, while records show that at the time she filed qualifying papers to run for city council, Ingram owned four properties in Mobile County, two of which are in District 2. Records also show that she owned several properties in Georgia.\nCar crashes into Crumbl cookie store in Mobile (photos) Reggie Hill, who was also a candidate, filed a lawsuit to have Ingram removed from the ballot prior to the election, but it was dismissed.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WKRG News 5.",
